## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates a candidate's ability to construct precise 3-valued assignments with exact unknown counts under Kleene semantics, along with a practical backtracking strategy for constraint satisfaction.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics
- Exact unknown count in constraint satisfaction
- Propagation and backtracking in three-valued logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v) {
  return v === 'T' ? 'F' : v === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U';
}

function imp(a, b) {
  return not(a) === 'T' ? b : not(a);
}

function evaluate(expr, vars) {
  if (Array.isArray(expr)) {
    const [op, left, right] = expr;
    const l = evaluate(left, vars);
    const r = evaluate(right, vars);
    if (op === 'and') return min(l, r);
    if (op === 'or') return max(l, r);
    if (op === 'imp') return imp(l, r);
  }
  return vars[expr];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design and reason about multi-cycle 3-valued constraint propagation, a common motif in policy engines and configuration management where Unknowns must be carefully propagated to avoid false constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic: T>U>F ordering, ¬T=F, ¬F=T, ¬U=U; ∨/∧ as max/min.
- Implication as a→b = ¬a ∨ b.
- Temporal constraints: handling two cycles and cross-time implications.
- Backtracking propagation: prune values that would force a constraint to F.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene three-valued helpers
const order = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function litOr(a,b){ return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b; }
function litAnd(a,b){ return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = not(a);
  return litOr(na, b);
}
```

